even as more and more nevadans get  vaccinated those who are not eligible  yet  feel left in the dark that includes  people with chronic health conditions  orco mana shares their concerns we have  been living in  intense and constant stress 32 year old  las vegas samantha russell has been  diagnosed with cancer  three times the disease combined with  treatment has taken its toll  on her immune system it does make people  like me  anxious the best protection against the  coronavirus  is the vaccine but according to the  state's vaccination playbook russell is  not eligible yet  it is hard to feel like  immunocompromised people or people with  disabilities  people like me are being prioritized  it's frustrating when  there are appointments available and  there are a lot of time slots open  this is a look at available appointments  this week at southern nevada health  district vaccine sites  we have a lot of capacity dr michael  gardner runs the unlv coveted 19 vaccine  clinics and says they're able to  administer  2 000 doses a day but they're not since  fewer people are signing up we're doing  maybe a little bit more than half of  what we could be doing we're afraid  that'll be the case until we get  some expansion of the lanes that were  allowed to vaccinate dr gardner says  he'd like to see nevadans with chronic  conditions added to the mix and russell  hopes that happens soon  just to know that we have that extra  layer of protection  will be the biggest weight lifted off of  our shoulders  orcomanna 8 news now now we reached out  to the state's health response team as  well as a southern nevada health  district to ask  when those with underlying conditions  could expect to become eligible  they have not gotten back to us just yet
 

